<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wali Songo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">15th–16th centuries</span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Wali Sanga</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Wali Songo, or 'nine saints', are the Muslim teachers revered in Javanese tradition for spreading <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a> in the 15th and 16th centuries. Among them were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-ampel">Sunan Ampel</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-giri">Sunan Giri</a>, Sunan Kalijaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunan-gunung-jati">Sunan Gunung Jati</a>; several founded religious schools or ruling houses, such as Giri and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cirebon">Cirebon</a>, and they supported the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/demak-sultanate">Demak Sultanate</a>. Their tombs on the north coast remain major pilgrimage sites.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wali-songo.jpg" alt="Wali Songo" width="320" height="203" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Greenland4 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Borobdur9205.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
